<% content_for :title, t(".title") %>

<%# 035 FR-002, US4: the account page holds two forms, one per thing to change,
    each with its own current-password check and its own submit. They used to
    be one form where a blank new password meant "keep the old one" — which made
    the password the least findable thing on the page that owns it.

    One column: both cards hold controls, so side by side they would break
    assert_tab_order_follows_visual_order (CLAUDE.md "Layout"). The title sits
    above the cards rather than inside the first one, which is no longer the
    whole page.

    The page used to borrow .auth-page from the signed-out screens — a 26rem
    column centred on its own, which on a desktop read as a narrow strip in the
    middle of a site that is 68rem wide everywhere else. It is a signed-in page
    like the others now: the main column, left-aligned, title flush left. The
    two cards about the reader's own account hang on the blue hinge; the one
    that deletes it on the destructive one (CLAUDE.md "The swap axis"). %>
<div class="stack">
  <div class="page-head">
    <h1 class="page-title"><%= t(".heading") %></h1>
  </div>

  <%= render "devise/registrations/email_form" %>
  <%= render "devise/registrations/password_form", password_change: @password_change %>

  <div class="card card--alert stack-tight">
    <h2 class="card-title"><%= t(".cancel_account_title") %></h2>
    <p class="card-lead"><%= t(".unhappy") %></p>
    <div class="row">
      <%= button_to t(".cancel_account_button"), registration_path(resource_name),
            data: { confirm: t(".cancel_account_confirm"), turbo_confirm: t(".cancel_account_confirm") },
            method: :delete, class: "btn btn-destructive" %>
      <%= link_to t(".back"), :back, class: "btn btn-secondary" %>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
